WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.400
Block two.

2
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:04.875
Two questions, and they turn out to be the same question.

3
00:00:04.875 --> 00:00:10.925
Who vouches for each claim in a record — and once you know that, how do you actually get the data out.

4
00:00:11.525 --> 00:00:17.000
Here is the distinction this whole block rests on, and it is subtler than it first appears.

5
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:22.275
These four words describe *sources of a claim*, not kinds of record.

6
00:00:22.275 --> 00:00:26.300
That matters, because one record routinely mixes them.

7
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:34.325
A single Swiss-Prot entry contains curated statements, experimental observations and predicted features on the same page.

8
00:00:34.325 --> 00:00:38.925
A PDB entry is both submitted and experimental at once.

9
00:00:38.925 --> 00:00:41.250
Taking them one at a time.

10
00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.900
Submitted means INSDC as deposited.

11
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:48.350
Somebody sent this in, and it is stored as sent.

12
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:55.875
Quality varies enormously, and there is a great deal of redundancy, because nothing is removed when something better arrives.

13
00:00:55.875 --> 00:01:01.850
Predicted means a program inferred it, from a genome or from similarity to something else.

14
00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:07.350
Curated means staff read the evidence, weighed it, and merged records accordingly.

15
00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:12.650
That is expensive, which is why there is so much less of it than of the alternatives.

16
00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:15.725
Experimental means somebody measured it.

17
00:01:15.725 --> 00:01:21.450
The summary sentence: databases differ less in what they store than in who vouches for it.

18
00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:26.450
When you open an unfamiliar database, that is the question to ask first.

19
00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:33.300
A word about why curation is rarer than you might expect, since it is obviously the most reliable of the four.

20
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:38.275
Curation is a person reading the literature and deciding what it supports.

21
00:01:38.275 --> 00:01:46.325
That takes an expert an appreciable amount of time per entry, and the number of experts does not grow when the number of sequences does.

22
00:01:46.325 --> 00:01:53.150
Swiss-Prot has been curated continuously since nineteen eighty-six and holds a few hundred thousand entries.

23
00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:57.525
In the same period, sequencing has produced hundreds of millions.

24
00:01:57.525 --> 00:02:02.675
So the fraction of our data that anybody has read is falling, and will keep falling.

25
00:02:02.675 --> 00:02:06.775
That is not a criticism of anyone; it is arithmetic.

26
00:02:06.775 --> 00:02:14.550
It does mean that "somebody has checked this" is an increasingly strong claim about a record, and worth noticing when it is true.

27
00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.475
Four claims on the slide.

28
00:02:17.475 --> 00:02:21.650
For each one, decide which of the four sources stands behind it.

29
00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:25.900
The sequence of that predicted protein record: predicted.

30
00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:29.500
And the record says so — MODEL REFSEQ.

31
00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:35.225
The atom positions in two H H B: experimental, from X-ray diffraction.

32
00:02:35.225 --> 00:02:40.625
But also submitted, because the depositors sent in the coordinates they refined.

33
00:02:40.625 --> 00:02:43.325
Both labels are correct at once.

34
00:02:43.325 --> 00:02:48.125
The function text in the Swiss-Prot entry for beta-globin: curated.

35
00:02:48.125 --> 00:02:53.825
A person read the literature and wrote that sentence, and there is a citation attached to it.

36
00:02:53.825 --> 00:02:58.625
A sequence as deposited in ENA: submitted, and nothing more.

37
00:02:58.625 --> 00:03:02.500
Nobody has vouched for it beyond the person who sent it.

38
00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:06.875
The second one is the interesting case, and I want to underline it.

39
00:03:06.875 --> 00:03:11.450
A record being experimental does not stop it being submitted.

40
00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:16.275
These categories overlap, and a claim can have more than one kind of backing.

41
00:03:16.275 --> 00:03:24.050
Asking "which one is it?" is the wrong question; asking "what is behind this, and how much does that support it?" is the right one.

42
00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:31.425
UniProt's knowledgebase has two sections, and the difference between them matters every single time you use it.

43
00:03:31.425 --> 00:03:34.125
Swiss-Prot is the reviewed section.

44
00:03:34.125 --> 00:03:40.900
Curators check the function, the domains and the literature, and there is exactly one entry per protein.

45
00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:45.025
If two submissions describe the same protein, they are merged.

46
00:03:45.025 --> 00:03:48.200
TrEMBL is the unreviewed section.

47
00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:55.025
Entries are annotated automatically, and most of them are translations of predicted coding sequences.

48
00:03:55.025 --> 00:03:57.225
Nobody has read them.

49
00:03:57.225 --> 00:04:02.500
One thing people consistently get wrong: these are not a subset and a superset.

50
00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:06.075
An entry sits in one or the other, never both.

51
00:04:06.075 --> 00:04:10.675
When a TrEMBL entry is reviewed, it *moves* into Swiss-Prot.

52
00:04:10.675 --> 00:04:13.825
And the sizes are not remotely comparable.

53
00:04:13.825 --> 00:04:19.450
Reviewed entries number in the hundreds of thousands; unreviewed in the hundreds of millions.

54
00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:29.375
That ratio has only ever moved in one direction since sequencing became cheap, and it will keep moving, because curation is done by people and sequencing is not.

55
00:04:29.375 --> 00:04:35.025
So when somebody says "it is in UniProt", the useful follow-up question is: which part?

56
00:04:35.625 --> 00:04:46.850
UniProt also grades how confident it is that the protein exists at all — which is a question most people never think to ask, and which has a surprising number of interesting answers.

57
00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:50.500
Every entry carries a protein existence level.

58
00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:59.075
Level one means protein-level evidence: somebody has detected the protein itself, by mass spectrometry or antibody or structure.

59
00:04:59.075 --> 00:05:05.000
Level two means transcript-level evidence — the RNA was seen, the protein inferred.

60
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:08.125
Level three is inferred from homology.

61
00:05:08.125 --> 00:05:10.675
Level four is predicted.

62
00:05:10.675 --> 00:05:13.275
Level five is uncertain.

63
00:05:13.275 --> 00:05:21.425
Beta-globin is level one, as you would hope for a protein that has been studied since the nineteen-fifties and crystallised hundreds of times.

64
00:05:21.425 --> 00:05:30.875
But for a protein from a newly sequenced genome, the level may well be four, and then the word "protein" in that record is doing a lot of optimistic work.

65
00:05:30.875 --> 00:05:35.825
It means: a gene-finding program thinks there is an open reading frame here.

66
00:05:35.825 --> 00:05:38.325
Nobody has seen the product.

67
00:05:38.325 --> 00:05:41.075
Why does this matter in practice?

68
00:05:41.075 --> 00:05:44.850
Because the existence level is a filter you can use.

69
00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:56.525
If you are building a reference set of proteins — to train something, to count something, to compare organisms — including level four and five entries means including things that may not exist.

70
00:05:56.525 --> 00:05:59.200
For some questions that is fine.

71
00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:09.650
For others it will systematically inflate your numbers, in a way that correlates with how well studied the organism is, which is exactly the kind of bias that is hard to see afterwards.

72
00:06:09.650 --> 00:06:12.525
UniProt lets you filter on it.

73
00:06:12.525 --> 00:06:15.850
Most people never do, and most people should.

74
00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:20.350
If you have a protein and one question about it, start here.

75
00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:39.250
The Swiss-Prot entry for beta-globin — accession P six eight eight seven one — gives you the function, the disease variants including sickle-cell anaemia, the post-translational modifications, the sequence features, and links out to essentially everything else: the gene, the transcripts, the structures, the families, the interactions.

76
00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:46.525
That makes it the best single entry point for a protein-level question, and it is where I would start nine times out of ten.

77
00:06:46.525 --> 00:06:49.325
One component we have not mentioned.

78
00:06:49.325 --> 00:06:58.500
UniProt has a third part, UniParc, which stores every unique protein sequence exactly once, under an identifier that is never reused.

79
00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:01.025
It is not somewhere you browse.

80
00:07:01.025 --> 00:07:10.275
It is how you recognise that a record in one database and a record in another hold literally the same sequence, even when neither of them cites the other.

81
00:07:10.275 --> 00:07:18.200
One more feature worth knowing about, because it saves an enormous amount of time: the evidence attached to individual statements.

82
00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:22.775
A Swiss-Prot entry does not simply assert that a protein does something.

83
00:07:22.775 --> 00:07:30.950
Beside each annotation there is a note of where that came from — a publication, an experiment, a similarity, an automatic rule.

84
00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:33.475
You can click through to the paper.

85
00:07:33.475 --> 00:07:38.300
So when you need to cite a protein's function, you do not cite the database.

86
00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:44.625
You follow the evidence to the paper and cite that, having checked that it says what the annotation claims.

87
00:07:44.625 --> 00:07:51.250
That is the difference between using a database and quoting it, and it is the habit this whole block is aiming at.

88
00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:57.200
Protein families are hierarchical, and InterPro is where you see the hierarchy.

89
00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:06.275
For beta-globin there are five nested entries, running from the globin superfamily at the top down to the beta-type globins specifically at the bottom.

90
00:08:06.275 --> 00:08:15.075
Which level you want depends entirely on your question — "is this a globin?" and "is this a beta-globin?" are different questions with different answers.

91
00:08:15.075 --> 00:08:27.225
The signatures come from about a dozen member databases, and InterPro's job is to reconcile them: where several member databases describe the same family, InterPro merges them into one entry.

92
00:08:27.225 --> 00:08:36.100
Most of those signatures are profile hidden Markov models, which is Session five's subject, so you will understand what is underneath them in two weeks.

93
00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:40.775
A piece of recent history worth knowing, because old links are everywhere.

94
00:08:40.775 --> 00:08:46.250
Pfam's own website was retired in twenty twenty-three and merged into InterPro.

95
00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:49.300
If you have a bookmark, it now sends you here.

96
00:08:49.300 --> 00:09:02.200
And for non-coding RNA, Rfam plays the equivalent role, using covariance models — which score base pairing together with the sequence, because for RNA the structure is often more conserved than the bases.

97
00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:06.150
Beta-globin has no Rfam entry, being a protein.

98
00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:13.350
Rfam's transfer RNA family is R F zero zero zero zero five, if you want to see what one looks like.

99
00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:18.650
A structure, and a number that describes the experiment rather than the model.

100
00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:27.900
Two H H B is human deoxyhaemoglobin, determined by X-ray diffraction, at a resolution of one point seven four ångström.

101
00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:30.850
Smaller numbers mean finer detail.

102
00:09:30.850 --> 00:09:35.625
Now, resolution is widely misunderstood, so let me be precise.

103
00:09:35.625 --> 00:09:42.225
It describes the *data*: how much detail the diffraction measurements could resolve.

104
00:09:42.225 --> 00:09:47.800
It does not describe the correctness of the atomic model that was built into those data.

105
00:09:47.800 --> 00:10:02.675
A model at one point seven four ångström is built on good data, and it can still have errors in it — particularly in flexible regions, in side chains at the surface, and in anything the crystallographer had to interpret rather than read off.

106
00:10:02.675 --> 00:10:07.075
So resolution tells you what was possible, not what was achieved.

107
00:10:07.075 --> 00:10:13.850
Beta-globin appears in hundreds of PDB entries, at various resolutions and in various states.

108
00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:23.075
Session six treats coordinates like these as data to be analysed, which is the right frame of mind: they are measurements with uncertainty, not facts.

109
00:10:23.075 --> 00:10:28.625
Two more things about a PDB entry that are easy to miss and change how you read it.

110
00:10:28.625 --> 00:10:35.275
The first is that what is deposited is a *model* fitted to data, plus the data themselves.

111
00:10:35.275 --> 00:10:38.200
The coordinates are an interpretation.

112
00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:44.475
For most of a well-resolved structure that interpretation is not in doubt; at the edges it can be.

113
00:10:44.475 --> 00:10:46.850
The second is the crystal.

114
00:10:46.850 --> 00:10:56.725
A protein in a crystal is at high concentration, often at low temperature, frequently with additives that helped it crystallise, and packed against copies of itself.

115
00:10:56.725 --> 00:10:59.650
Most of the time this does not matter.

116
00:10:59.650 --> 00:11:07.725
Occasionally it does — a loop is held in place by a neighbouring molecule, or a conformation is selected that is rare in solution.

117
00:11:07.725 --> 00:11:17.450
So a structure is a snapshot of one state under one set of conditions, which is exactly the point Session six returns to with haemoglobin's T and R forms.

118
00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:22.225
And here is the same protein, predicted rather than measured.

119
00:11:22.225 --> 00:11:30.900
The AlphaFold database entry carries a confidence score called pLDDT, given per residue on a scale from zero to a hundred.

120
00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:36.100
It is the model's own estimate of how right it is about each part of the structure.

121
00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:41.850
The bands are worth memorising, because you will use them constantly from Session twelve onwards.

122
00:11:41.850 --> 00:11:46.375
Above ninety, the model is accurate down to the side-chain positions.

123
00:11:46.375 --> 00:11:50.925
Above seventy, the backbone is usually right even if details are not.

124
00:11:50.925 --> 00:12:01.100
Below fifty, confidence is very low — and one common reason for that is intrinsic disorder, where the protein may have no single structure for the model to be right about.

125
00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:08.100
A low pLDDT is sometimes a correct statement about biology rather than a failure of prediction.

126
00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:12.900
AlphaFold DB covers essentially every protein in UniProt.

127
00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:17.250
That is a sentence that would have been science fiction in twenty nineteen.

128
00:12:17.850 --> 00:12:22.800
Both are numbers attached to a structure, and they mean entirely different things.

129
00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:28.200
I labour this because the two get put side by side in tables, and they should not be.

130
00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:34.475
Resolution is a property of experimental data — a statement about an instrument and a crystal.

131
00:12:34.475 --> 00:12:41.150
pLDDT is a model's opinion of itself — a statement about a neural network's confidence.

132
00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:48.125
They are not comparable, they are not convertible, and a structure with a good pLDDT has not been measured at all.

133
00:12:48.125 --> 00:12:56.525
For beta-globin, with hundreds of experimental structures available, the predicted model adds very little; you would use the experiment.

134
00:12:56.525 --> 00:13:03.825
AlphaFold DB matters most for the enormous number of proteins that have no structure at all, which is most of them.

135
00:13:03.825 --> 00:13:11.625
There is a third number you will meet alongside these, and it is worth naming now so it does not confuse you later: the B-factor.

136
00:13:11.625 --> 00:13:18.675
In an experimental structure it describes how much an atom moves, or how poorly it is pinned down by the data.

137
00:13:18.675 --> 00:13:25.400
In an AlphaFold model, the field in the file that would hold the B-factor holds the pLDDT instead.

138
00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:32.250
So the same column of the same file format means two unrelated things depending on where the file came from.

139
00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:39.125
Software that averages them together will produce a number that means nothing at all, and such software exists.

140
00:13:39.125 --> 00:13:40.900
Read the header.

141
00:13:40.900 --> 00:13:43.325
The file says which it is.

142
00:13:43.925 --> 00:13:51.000
Interaction databases differ in how direct their evidence is, and the range is wider than most people assume.

143
00:13:51.000 --> 00:14:00.350
At the most direct end: a curated record of a physical interaction that somebody measured, by co-immunoprecipitation or two-hybrid or structure.

144
00:14:00.350 --> 00:14:12.925
At the least direct end: an association inferred from indirect signals — the two proteins are co-expressed, or co-mentioned in the literature, or their genes sit next to each other in many bacterial genomes.

145
00:14:12.925 --> 00:14:18.725
IntAct, part of the IMEx consortium, holds curated experimental interactions.

146
00:14:18.725 --> 00:14:28.625
STRING answers a deliberately broader question — which proteins are functionally associated — by combining experimental and predicted evidence into a single score.

147
00:14:28.625 --> 00:14:37.850
FunCoup, built here at Stockholm University, constructs a comparable network by a different route, and a later course in the programme covers it properly.

148
00:14:37.850 --> 00:14:40.475
The thing to carry away is this.

149
00:14:40.475 --> 00:14:48.500
A line between two proteins in a network diagram can mean somebody watched them bind, or it can mean they appear in the same papers.

150
00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:52.725
Those are very different claims, and the picture looks identical.

151
00:14:52.725 --> 00:14:56.425
Always check which you are looking at before you build on it.

152
00:14:57.025 --> 00:15:05.450
A short slide to close the protein tour, and it is the same caution as the RNA expression slide one block ago, moved up a level.

153
00:15:05.450 --> 00:15:11.625
Knowing a protein's sequence tells you nothing about how much of it is present, or where in the cell it is.

154
00:15:11.625 --> 00:15:13.625
Those are measurements.

155
00:15:13.625 --> 00:15:23.400
The Human Protein Atlas is where to look for them in human: tissues, cell lines, subcellular location, with microscopy images and abundance estimates.

156
00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:30.575
The microscopy can show you which compartment a protein sits in, which is information no sequence record carries.

157
00:15:30.575 --> 00:15:34.175
And keep RNA and protein measurements apart.

158
00:15:34.175 --> 00:15:43.050
Translation and degradation both sit between the transcript and the protein, so RNA abundance and protein abundance can genuinely differ.

159
00:15:43.050 --> 00:15:49.900
Comparing amounts also needs the method and the samples, not just two numbers that happen to be in the same units.

160
00:15:49.900 --> 00:15:55.875
If somebody asks you "is this protein in blood", the sequence record does not answer that.

161
00:15:55.875 --> 00:16:01.000
It is a perfectly reasonable question and it belongs somewhere else entirely.

162
00:16:01.600 --> 00:16:05.100
Here is the whole thread of the lecture in one picture.

163
00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:13.775
Each row is a different database's record of the same underlying biology — the gene, the transcript, the protein, the structure, the complex.

164
00:16:13.775 --> 00:16:19.550
And each one is reached from the row above it by following a cross-reference, not by searching again.

165
00:16:19.550 --> 00:16:23.900
That is the practical demonstration of the first of our two habits.

166
00:16:23.900 --> 00:16:26.500
You type a name once, at the top.

167
00:16:26.500 --> 00:16:29.550
Everything after that is navigation.

168
00:16:29.550 --> 00:16:38.725
The notebook fetches every one of these records live, so what you see on the slide is what the databases say today, not what they said when the slide was made.

169
00:16:38.725 --> 00:16:44.075
If something has changed since this was recorded, the notebook will show you the change.

170
00:16:44.075 --> 00:16:48.425
The lab ends with a dossier of your own, for human myoglobin.

171
00:16:49.025 --> 00:16:53.500
A practical point about searching that will save you a great deal of time.

172
00:16:53.500 --> 00:17:05.400
An everything-at-once search — the single box on a database's front page — returns genome records, gene records, transcript records, protein records and literature, mixed together in one list.

173
00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:11.225
You then have to sort them out by eye, and the categories are not always obvious from the titles.

174
00:17:11.225 --> 00:17:19.250
The same words typed into NCBI Gene, or into UniProt specifically, give you a short list of exactly one kind of thing.

175
00:17:19.250 --> 00:17:25.475
So the habit is: decide which biological level you want *before* you search, not after.

176
00:17:25.475 --> 00:17:29.600
It turns a page of mixed results into a handful of candidates.

177
00:17:29.600 --> 00:17:34.350
There is a second reason to narrow before you search, beyond saving time.

178
00:17:34.350 --> 00:17:39.200
A specific collection lets you filter in ways the general search cannot.

179
00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:43.050
In NCBI Gene you can restrict to an organism.

180
00:17:43.050 --> 00:17:46.650
In UniProt you can restrict to reviewed entries.

181
00:17:46.650 --> 00:17:53.350
Those filters do not exist on the front page, because the front page does not know what kind of thing you are looking for.

182
00:17:53.350 --> 00:17:56.650
And a warning about the word "human" in a query.

183
00:17:56.650 --> 00:18:07.100
Typing it does not filter by organism — it searches for the word, which appears in the descriptions of records from every organism that has ever been compared to a human one.

184
00:18:07.100 --> 00:18:09.250
You will get zebrafish.

185
00:18:09.250 --> 00:18:11.825
Use the organism filter.

186
00:18:12.425 --> 00:18:16.650
And here is the version of all this that you can actually repeat.

187
00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:25.500
NCBI's E-utilities, reached through Bio dot Entrez in Biopython; the UniProt REST API; the PDB REST API.

188
00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:32.675
All three let you fetch records from a script, and the course notebook builds the entire beta-globin dossier that way.

189
00:18:32.675 --> 00:18:36.425
Two advantages over clicking, and they are both large.

190
00:18:36.425 --> 00:18:39.225
The first is reproducibility.

191
00:18:39.225 --> 00:18:42.350
The script *is* the record of what you did.

192
00:18:42.350 --> 00:18:47.825
There is no ambiguity about which record you fetched or when, because the code says so.

193
00:18:47.825 --> 00:18:49.900
The second is scale.

194
00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:54.175
The same four lines that fetch one protein fetch four hundred.

195
00:18:54.175 --> 00:19:03.250
Clicking does not scale at all, and the moment your question involves more than a handful of records, the script is not the harder route — it is the easier one.

196
00:19:03.850 --> 00:19:06.125
You have an identifier.

197
00:19:06.125 --> 00:19:11.575
How you retrieve the record depends on what you intend to do with it, and there are three routes.

198
00:19:11.575 --> 00:19:19.250
A record page is right when you want to read one entry — follow its evidence, see its cross-references, download its sequence.

199
00:19:19.250 --> 00:19:24.200
The UniProt beta-globin page is the example we have been using all morning.

200
00:19:24.200 --> 00:19:31.575
A command or a script is right when the retrieval has to be repeated, or when the result feeds straight into an analysis.

201
00:19:31.575 --> 00:19:39.850
An application programming interface — an API — is the documentation that tells you how to ask and which formats you can have back.

202
00:19:39.850 --> 00:19:45.675
And a genome browser is right when the question is where something lies, and what sits next to it.

203
00:19:45.675 --> 00:19:48.475
All three reach the same records.

204
00:19:48.475 --> 00:19:55.675
The browser is the one you have used least and the one the lab leans on hardest, so the rest of this block is spent there.

205
00:19:56.275 --> 00:20:05.525
Here is the beta-globin locus in the UCSC Genome Browser, on GRCh38 — hg38 in UCSC's own naming.

206
00:20:05.525 --> 00:20:08.875
Several things in this view are worth pointing out.

207
00:20:08.875 --> 00:20:15.425
The ruler across the top gives the coordinates, and every track below it is drawn against that same ruler.

208
00:20:15.425 --> 00:20:24.325
That is the whole idea of a browser: separate kinds of information, forced onto one coordinate system, so that you can see what lines up with what.

209
00:20:24.325 --> 00:20:27.725
The transcripts appear as blocks joined by lines.

210
00:20:27.725 --> 00:20:31.175
The blocks are exons and the lines are introns.

211
00:20:31.175 --> 00:20:37.250
Where a block is thick, that is coding sequence; where it is thin, that is untranslated region.

212
00:20:37.250 --> 00:20:45.400
And the small chevrons point left, because HBB is transcribed from the reverse strand — towards lower reference coordinates.

213
00:20:45.400 --> 00:20:51.825
Three transcript annotations are stacked here: GENCODE, curated RefSeq, and MANE Select.

214
00:20:51.825 --> 00:20:58.625
They are different annotation projects describing the same DNA, which is useful precisely when they disagree.

215
00:20:58.625 --> 00:21:02.500
The thing I actually want you to notice is the title bar.

216
00:21:02.500 --> 00:21:08.750
It carries the region and the assembly, which is exactly and completely what a retrieval log needs.

217
00:21:08.750 --> 00:21:14.125
Copy that one line into your notes and somebody else can return to the identical view.

218
00:21:14.125 --> 00:21:15.825
One warning.

219
00:21:15.825 --> 00:21:21.975
What you can see depends on which tracks are switched on, how they are displayed, and what filters are applied.

220
00:21:21.975 --> 00:21:29.125
If something you expected is not there, it may be hidden, it may be filtered out, or you may need to zoom in further.

221
00:21:29.125 --> 00:21:33.475
Absence in a browser window is not absence from the database.

222
00:21:34.075 --> 00:21:37.975
A colleague asks you for the sequence of human HBB.

223
00:21:37.975 --> 00:21:43.175
This slide is the reason you should ask what they plan to do with it before you send anything.

224
00:21:43.175 --> 00:21:49.550
Start from the reference transcript, N M underscore zero zero zero five one eight point five.

225
00:21:49.550 --> 00:21:53.300
There are four different sequences you might reasonably mean.

226
00:21:53.300 --> 00:21:58.575
The genomic span of that transcript is one thousand six hundred and eight bases.

227
00:21:58.575 --> 00:22:05.850
That covers the exons and the introns between them, and it is what you need if the question concerns an intronic variant.

228
00:22:05.850 --> 00:22:09.850
The spliced transcript is six hundred and twenty-eight bases.

229
00:22:09.850 --> 00:22:14.525
The exons joined together, with both untranslated ends still on.

230
00:22:14.525 --> 00:22:17.750
That is what you need to see which exons meet.

231
00:22:17.750 --> 00:22:25.650
If the download is labelled cDNA, it is this sequence written with DNA letters — a T wherever the RNA has a U.

232
00:22:25.650 --> 00:22:31.750
The coding sequence is four hundred and forty-four bases: start codon through stop codon.

233
00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:38.275
The remaining one hundred and eighty-four bases of the spliced transcript are the untranslated regions.

234
00:22:38.275 --> 00:22:46.850
And the protein, N P underscore zero zero zero five zero nine point one, is one hundred and forty-seven amino acids.

235
00:22:46.850 --> 00:22:52.050
Now do that last piece of arithmetic, because it is the one that catches people.

236
00:22:52.050 --> 00:23:00.300
Four hundred and forty-four divided by three is one hundred and forty-eight, and the protein is one hundred and forty-seven residues.

237
00:23:00.300 --> 00:23:03.250
The missing triplet is the stop codon.

238
00:23:03.250 --> 00:23:07.975
It is part of the coding sequence and it codes for no amino acid.

239
00:23:07.975 --> 00:23:10.625
Four sequences, four lengths.

240
00:23:10.625 --> 00:23:20.250
The FASTA header and the length together tell you which one you were actually sent — which is the cheapest check in this entire lecture, and it costs you one glance.

241
00:23:20.850 --> 00:23:23.450
The view you saw is not fixed.

242
00:23:23.450 --> 00:23:27.650
A browser is a set of answers waiting for you to choose the question.

243
00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:31.400
Start by keeping what you need and hiding what you do not.

244
00:23:31.400 --> 00:23:37.900
We wanted to see where the exons lie, so the curated RefSeq transcript stays and the rest goes.

245
00:23:37.900 --> 00:23:45.200
Where a track offers both modes, dense squashes it into a single row and pack keeps the individual features labelled.

246
00:23:45.200 --> 00:23:48.350
Then change the question and change the tracks.

247
00:23:48.350 --> 00:23:52.000
Which parts of this region are conserved across species?

248
00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:59.775
Stay at the same coordinates, add a conservation track, and compare the signal over the exons against the DNA around them.

249
00:23:59.775 --> 00:24:02.725
Where was RNA actually detected?

250
00:24:02.725 --> 00:24:07.525
That is an RNA sequencing coverage track, for a particular tissue.

251
00:24:07.525 --> 00:24:11.425
Which DNA was accessible in a particular cell type?

252
00:24:11.425 --> 00:24:13.950
That is a regulation track.

253
00:24:13.950 --> 00:24:19.300
In every case the track description tells you which species, which tissue, which assay.

254
00:24:19.300 --> 00:24:25.375
Read it, because "there is a signal here" means nothing until you know what was measured and in what.

255
00:24:25.375 --> 00:24:32.500
UCSC groups these by type and gives you Track Search to find one by name; Ensembl has its Track panel.

256
00:24:32.500 --> 00:24:40.700
And one caution for when you start treating two tracks as independent confirmation: tracks can draw on the same underlying dataset.

257
00:24:40.700 --> 00:24:44.625
Agreement between them is then not evidence of anything.

258
00:24:45.225 --> 00:24:52.925
Put ClinVar beside the curated RefSeq transcript and two variants show up in HBB, in quite different places.

259
00:24:52.925 --> 00:24:55.850
The first falls in a coding exon.

260
00:24:55.850 --> 00:25:03.250
In transcript coordinates it is c dot twenty A to T, and it replaces a glutamate with a valine in beta-globin.

261
00:25:03.250 --> 00:25:11.850
The same change, described against the chromosome, is g dot five two two seven zero zero two T to A on GRCh38.

262
00:25:11.850 --> 00:25:14.350
Look at those two descriptions.

263
00:25:14.350 --> 00:25:17.800
One says A to T and the other says T to A.

264
00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:19.950
That is not an error.

265
00:25:19.950 --> 00:25:27.375
The c dot description follows the transcript, and the g dot description follows the forward strand of the chromosome reference.

266
00:25:27.375 --> 00:25:32.575
HBB is on the reverse strand, so the two descriptions are complementary.

267
00:25:32.575 --> 00:25:38.125
It is the same single change, written down twice, against two different references.

268
00:25:38.125 --> 00:25:42.750
The second variant is c dot ninety-three minus twenty-one G to A.

269
00:25:42.750 --> 00:25:48.425
The minus tells you it is intronic — twenty-one bases before coding base ninety-three.

270
00:25:48.425 --> 00:25:56.525
It is not in the spliced transcript at all, which is exactly why your colleague needed the genomic span rather than the cDNA.

271
00:25:56.525 --> 00:26:01.800
And a change inside an intron can still move where the cell splices the RNA.

272
00:26:01.800 --> 00:26:08.425
From the position on screen, the ClinVar record takes you to the submissions and the publications behind it.

273
00:26:08.425 --> 00:26:15.150
That is the hinge into block three: the browser can show you where a variant is, and it cannot tell you what it does.

274
00:26:15.150 --> 00:26:17.800
Only an experiment does that.

275
00:26:18.400 --> 00:26:24.925
Your colleague opens Ensembl instead, and finds HBB in the same region of chromosome eleven.

276
00:26:24.925 --> 00:26:28.300
The controls look nothing like UCSC's.

277
00:26:28.300 --> 00:26:30.375
That does not matter.

278
00:26:30.375 --> 00:26:37.250
Assembly, coordinates and strand tell you whether you are in the same place, and those three travel between browsers.

279
00:26:37.250 --> 00:26:42.850
What you also need, to compare two downloads, is whether the selected transcripts match.

280
00:26:42.850 --> 00:26:46.150
The MANE Select label is what gives you that.

281
00:26:46.150 --> 00:27:04.000
MANE is an agreement between NCBI and EMBL-EBI on one representative transcript per human gene, and here it ties Ensembl's E N S T zero zero zero zero zero three three five two nine five point four to RefSeq's N M underscore zero zero zero five one eight point five.

282
00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:08.700
Matching exon coordinates, matching coding region, matching sequence.

283
00:27:08.700 --> 00:27:13.925
So if your file and your colleague's file differ, you have somewhere specific to look.

284
00:27:13.925 --> 00:27:16.550
One of you included introns.

285
00:27:16.550 --> 00:27:19.475
One of you exported a wider region.

286
00:27:19.475 --> 00:27:22.375
One of you took the other orientation.

287
00:27:22.375 --> 00:27:27.150
Those are the three usual answers, and the retrieval log tells you which.

